## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128-bit values without hardware helpers.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit representation as hi/lo
- Leading-zero counting
- Branching vs branchless approaches
- Correct handling of zero input

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo helper assuming a 64-bit lz routine exists as lz64
static int lz64(uint64_t v){
  int n = 0;
  if ((v & 0xFFFFFFFF00000000ULL) == 0) { n += 32; v <<= 32; }
  if ((v & 0xFFFF000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 16; v <<= 16; }
  if ((v & 0xFF00000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 8; v <<= 8; }
  if ((v & 0xF000000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 4; v <<= 4; }
  if ((v & 0xC000000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 2; v <<= 2; }
  if ((v & 0x8000000000000000ULL) == 0) { n += 1; }
  return n;
}
int countLeadingZeros128(uint128 x){
  if (x.hi != 0) return lz64(x.hi);
  if (x.lo != 0) return 64 + lz64(x.lo);
  return 128;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement countTrailingZeros128 without 128-bit intrinsics?
- How would you adapt this for a language without unsigned 64-bit types?

## Why This Is Asked
Transposition in bit-sliced form is a classic challenge that tests mastery of layout tricks and how data moves across a 128-bit container without branches.

## Key Concepts
- Bit-slice layout and Morton-order ideas
- Multi-stage bit redistribution with masks
- Branchless, vector-style bit manipulation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation outline for guidance
uint128 transpose8x16(uint128 x) {
  // stage 1: swap adjacent bits
  x = (x & 0x5555...5555) | ((x >> 1) & 0x5555...5555);
  // stage 2: swap pairs
  x = (x & 0x3333...3333) | ((x >> 2) & 0x3333...3333);
  // stage 3: swap nibbles
  x = (x & 0x0F0F...0F0F) | ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F...0F0F);
  // stage 4: move bytes
  x = (x & 0x00FF00FF) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF);
  return x;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

New angle: checks two-bit pair occurrences across 128 bits without branches, emphasizing careful cross-half packing.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit lane partitioning; - mask-based pair extraction; - correct half packing of results.

## Code Example

```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint128 twoBitsSetMask128(uint128 x){
  const uint64_t M = 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  uint64_t loMask = (x.lo & M) & ((x.lo >> 1) & M);
  uint64_t hiMask = (x.hi & M) & ((x.hi >> 1) & M);
  uint128 r; r.lo = loMask; r.hi = (hiMask & 0xFFFFFFFFULL) << 32; return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to 3-bit groups across 192 bits?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of 128-bit arithmetic using hi/lo, avoiding loops, and applying a scalable, branchless popcount.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit representation via hi/lo
- SWAR/parallel bit counting
- Endianness and safe casting between types

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x){
  x -= (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

uint8_t popcount128(uint128 x){
  return (uint8_t)(popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo));
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to accumulate using wider accumulators? 
- What is the performance impact on platforms with native popcount intrinsics?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests precise bit permutation across 128-bit boundary without loops. Requires careful cross-half carry handling when shifting hi/lo.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit value as {hi, lo}
- Masks for even/odd bit positions across both halves
- Cross-half carry during shifts between lo and hi

## Code Example

```javascript
// Outline (not full implementation)
uint128 swapAdjacentBits128(uint128 x) {
  uint128 evenMask = {0x5555555555555555ULL, 0x5555555555555555ULL};
  uint128 oddMask  = {0xAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAULL, 0xAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAULL};
  uint64_t lo_e = x.lo & evenMask.lo; uint64_t hi_e = x.hi & evenMask.hi;
  uint64_t lo_o = x.lo & oddMask.lo;  uint64_t hi_o = x.hi & oddMask.hi;
  uint64_t lo_left  = (lo_e << 1);
  uint64_t hi_left  = (hi_e << 1) | (lo_e >> 63);
  uint64_t lo_right = (lo_o >> 1);
  uint64_t hi_right = (hi_o >> 1);
  uint128 res;
  res.lo = lo_left | lo_right;
  res.hi = hi_left | hi_right;
  // handle cross-half carry from lo_o/hi_o if needed
  return res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to arbitrary bit widths or detect overflow across the 128-bit boundary?
- How would you unit-test edge cases: all zeros, all ones, alternating patterns, and patterns crossing the boundary?

